# 1. In:
# A text sentence typed by the user.

# 2. Process:
# Applies four string transformation methods (.upper(), .lower(), .title(), and .replace()) to format the input string.

# 3. Out:
# Prints each of the four transformed strings on individual lines in the output terminal.

# 4. My four transformations, and when each is useful:
# - .upper(): Useful for creating bold headlines or emphasizing urgent notifications.
# - .lower(): Useful for normalizing user inputs (like email addresses) before storing or comparing them.
# - .title(): Useful for correctly formatting names, article titles, or proper nouns.
# - .replace(" ", "_"): Useful for generating valid file names, database keys, or URL slugs from plain text.

# Observation Comments for "CHECK IT YOURSELF":
# Sentence tested: "  hello World  "
# - .upper() produced "  HELLO WORLD  ": Preserved extra spaces and converted all characters to uppercase as expected.
# - .lower() produced "  hello world  ": Preserved extra spaces and converted the capitalized 'W' to lowercase as expected.
# - .title() produced "  Hello World  ": Preserved outer spaces and capitalized 'Hello' while keeping 'World' title-cased as expected.
# - .replace(" ", "_") produced "__hello_World__": Replaced every single space (including leading/trailing spaces) with an underscore as expected.


# Your code below

user_sentence = input("Enter a sentence: ")

print("1. Uppercase:", user_sentence.upper())
print("2. Lowercase:", user_sentence.lower())
print("3. Title Case:", user_sentence.title())
print("4. Underscores:", user_sentence.replace(" ", "_"))